# Week 12 — July 23

## Skill 17 — Lab: Configuring IPv6 at Castle Rysen

### Lab Objective

Configure IPv6 on the **Cafe router** and **Fallout Shelter router**, establish the IPv6 WAN connection, configure IPv6 static routes between the sites, and verify end-to-end IPv6 connectivity.

---

# 1. Initial IPv4 State

Before configuring IPv6, the Fallout Shelter router `FO-RT01` had the following IPv4 interfaces:

```text
FastEthernet0/0.10     10.0.16.2
FastEthernet0/0.20     10.0.16.130
FastEthernet0/0.30     10.0.17.2
FastEthernet0/0.40     10.0.17.130
FastEthernet0/1        216.0.5.2
Serial0/0/0            172.16.0.1
```

The Cafe router `cafe01-RT01` had:

```text
GigabitEthernet0/0.10  10.0.18.1
GigabitEthernet0/0.20  10.0.18.33
Serial0/1/0            172.16.0.2
```

The existing WAN connection was:

```text
FO-RT01                  cafe01-RT01
172.16.0.1 ------------- 172.16.0.2
       Serial WAN
```

---

# 2. Enable IPv6 Routing

IPv6 unicast routing was enabled on `FO-RT01`:

```cisco
conf t
ipv6 unicast-routing
```

This allows the router to perform IPv6 routing rather than simply operating as an IPv6-enabled host.

The same configuration was applied to `cafe01-RT01`:

```cisco
conf t
ipv6 unicast-routing
```

---

# 3. Configure IPv6 on Fallout Shelter VLANs

The Fallout Shelter has four IPv6 VLAN networks.

| Interface | IPv6 Network        | Method |
| --------- | ------------------- | ------ |
| Fa0/0.10  | `2001:DB8:1:4::/64` | EUI-64 |
| Fa0/0.20  | `2001:DB8:1:5::/64` | EUI-64 |
| Fa0/0.30  | `2001:DB8:1:6::/64` | EUI-64 |
| Fa0/0.40  | `2001:DB8:1:7::/64` | EUI-64 |

Configuration:

```cisco
interface fastEthernet 0/0.10
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:4::/64 eui-64

interface fastEthernet 0/0.20
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:5::/64 eui-64

interface fastEthernet 0/0.30
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:6::/64 eui-64

interface fastEthernet 0/0.40
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:7::/64 eui-64
```

---

# 4. EUI-64 Result

After configuration, `FO-RT01` automatically generated interface IDs from its MAC address.

The same link-local address appeared on the interfaces:

```text
FE80::201:63FF:FEDC:D001
```

The global addresses became:

```text
Fa0/0.10
2001:DB8:1:4:201:63FF:FEDC:D001

Fa0/0.20
2001:DB8:1:5:201:63FF:FEDC:D001

Fa0/0.30
2001:DB8:1:6:201:63FF:FEDC:D001

Fa0/0.40
2001:DB8:1:7:201:63FF:FEDC:D001
```

### Important observation

The `/64` prefix was manually supplied:

```text
2001:DB8:1:4::/64
```

while EUI-64 generated:

```text
201:63FF:FEDC:D001
```

So the resulting address is:

```text
2001:DB8:1:4:201:63FF:FEDC:D001
         └──────────────┘
          EUI-64 ID
```

