# 5. Configure IPv6 on the WAN

Initially, the WAN interface was configured using EUI-64:

```cisco
interface s0/0/0
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:3::2/64 eui-64
```

This produced:

```text
2001:DB8:1:3:201:63FF:FEDC:D001
```

However, this was then removed:

```cisco
no ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:3::2/64 eui-64
```

and replaced with a manually assigned address:

```cisco
ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:3::2/64
```

### Final Fallout Shelter WAN address

```text
2001:DB8:1:3::2/64
```

This is an important practical decision from the lesson: **the point-to-point WAN addresses were manually assigned so that the next-hop addresses are predictable and easy to reference in static routes.**

---

# 6. Configure IPv6 on Cafe Router

The Cafe router uses two VLANs.

| Interface | IPv6 Address         |
| --------- | -------------------- |
| Gi0/0.10  | `2001:DB8:1:1::1/64` |
| Gi0/0.20  | `2001:DB8:1:2::1/64` |

Configuration:

```cisco
interface gigabitEthernet 0/0.10
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:1::1/64

interface gigabitEthernet 0/0.20
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:2::1/64
```

Unlike the Fallout Shelter VLAN interfaces, these use **explicit interface IDs** rather than EUI-64.

---

# 7. Configure Cafe WAN Interface

The Cafe router's Serial interface was configured with:

```cisco
interface s0/1/0
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:1:3::1/64
```

The final IPv6 WAN link is therefore:

```text
cafe01-RT01                         FO-RT01
2001:DB8:1:3::1 ================== 2001:DB8:1:3::2
                 IPv6 WAN
```

Both interfaces belong to:

```text
2001:DB8:1:3::/64
```

